  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 126 AM MDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nThe upper level pattern will remain relatively unchanged today, \nwith the upper level high off to our southeast and broad troughing\noff the West Coast. A weak disturbance will move through the flow\naloft over the area this afternoon. In addition to this, the \nupper level jet will be moving off to the northeast, which could \nput us in the right entrance region for a brief time this \nafternoon. Both of these things will provide upper level support \nfor shower and thunderstorm development. At the surface, easterly \nwinds in the afternoon will allow for upslope flow and a continued\ninflow of surface moisture. All of these means we're looking at \nanother day of scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms. \nThe main threat again today will be heavy rainfall leading to \nflash flooding (which is discussed further in the hydrology \nsection of this AFD), but we also have the potential for severe \nweather. 0-6km shear will increase for this afternoon, with values\naround 30 to 45kts. Instability will be decent, with steep low to\nmid level lapse rates and MLCAPEs exceeding 1000 J/kg. Both large\nhail and strong winds will be possible with the stronger storms \nthis afternoon. Shower and thunderstorm activity should start to \nweaken and exit our eastern plains around 10PM.\n\nThe upper level high will start to push more west on Sunday, turning \nwinds aloft more zonal rather than southwest. This will bring some \ndrier air aloft and our PWs will drop a bit closer to normal, but \nstill remain elevated. Easterly surface winds will allow for some\nupslope. Instability and shear will be similar to this afternoon,\nif the morning low clouds scatter out fast enough for surface \nheating. However, if the low clouds stick around longer than \ncurrently forecast, we could struggle to tap into the higher \ninstability. Storm coverage looks a bit lower on Sunday compared \nto today and yesterday, but we could still see some scattered \nactivity with a few storms going strong to severe. Heavy rain, \nstrong winds, and hail will all be threats with this activity. \n\nDrier and warmer conditions are expected for Monday and Tuesday \nas that upper high moves closer to the area. Highs on Monday will\nbe near normal, with highs on Tuesday reaching back into the low \n90s or much of the plains. Models are hinting at a weak cold front\nand shortwave on Wednesday, which could bring temperatures down a\nfew degrees and bring back our rain chances. The upper level high\nwill start to build more north to the west of our area, putting \nus in northwest flow aloft. This pattern will favor near to \nslightly above normal temperatures and potentially some isolated \nstorms in the afternoons through the end of the work week.",
